=== Ablated Response (multiplier=0.0) ===
<think>
Okay, I need to explain how the immune system works. Let me start by recalling what I know. The immune system is our body's defense against pathogens like bacteria, viruses, fungi, etc. It has two main branches: innate and adaptive. 

First, the innate immune system. I remember it's the first line of defense, non-specific. It includes physical barriers like skin and mucus. Then there are chemical barriers, maybe things like stomach acid. Then cells and molecules. Neutrophils, white blood cells, macrophages, dendritic cells. They recognize pathogens through pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) that detect PAMPs—pathogen-associated molecular patterns, which are common in many pathogens. 

Then there's the humoral response, which is antibodies. B cells produce antibodies when they're activated by antigens. T cells are another part. There are helper T cells (CD4+), cytotoxic T cells (CD8+), regulatory T cells. Helper T cells help other cells, like B cells and macrophages. Cytotoxic T cells kill infected or cancerous cells. Regulatory T cells prevent over-reaction.

The adaptive immune system is more specific. It develops memory after exposure. When a pathogen is encountered again, the response is faster and stronger. So it involves antigen presentation by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) like dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells. Antigen-presenting cells take up antigens, process them into peptides, present them on MHC molecules. T cells recognize these peptides-MHC complexes. 

B cells bind to antigens with their B cell receptors (BCRs), become activated, proliferate, differentiate into plasma cells (which make antibodies) and memory B cells. Helper T cells, recognizing the same peptide-MHC complex, release cytokines to help B cells and macrophages. Macrophages and dendritic cells, after being activated, secrete cytokines to enhance their activity and recruit more immune cells.

There's also the role of inflammation. When pathogens are detected, the immune system releases cytokines that cause redness, swelling, heat, pain— inflammation—to recruit more immune cells. But too much inflammation can be harmful.

I should also mention the difference between innate and adaptive. Innate is immediate but non-specific, adaptive is slower but specific with memory. Maybe touch on the components: cells (neutrophils, monocytes, lymphocytes), molecules (antibodies, cytokines, complement system).

